※ pl4g-test: compile=ok opt=0,1
※ pl4g-test: run=native,qemu exit=7
※ pl4g-test: diag=none

※ Compilation covers the whole program, so a function that is not exported and
※ that nothing reaches is one nothing can ever call.  It is left out of the
※ binary entirely; the program says the same thing without it.
let g: mut u8 = 0u8

※ Reached, because the entry point calls the constructors itself.
@[constructor, impure]
fn prepare():
    g ← 7u8

※ Not reached from anywhere, and not exported.
fn unreached() → u8:
    2u8

@[startup]
fn main() → u8:
    g
